Specification Books

Description The book: one file, one chart of accounts, one set of transactions.
Description There are two of them here, a business one and a personal one, and
Description nothing joins them.
Details
Details Every other specification in this folder is written about one book and
Details says so by saying nothing: "the chart of accounts" means the chart of
Details accounts of the book being worked in. This file is what makes that
Details precise, now that there are two.
Details
Details Two books rather than one set of accounts with a business branch is the
Details decision. A sole proprietor files a business return and a personal one,
Details and the figures on each must come from somewhere that cannot
Details accidentally include the other. Separate files give that by
Details construction: there is no query that can reach across them, so no
Details report can be wrong in that particular way.

Entity Book
Description One book, which is one file on disk.
| Attribute        | Type        | Default    | Notes                           |
| Name             | Text        |            | What the user calls it          |
| FilePath         | Text        |            | Where it is stored              |
| FiscalYearStart  | Integer     | 1          | Month; see the rule in Reports  |
| FirstDate        | Date        | 2005-01-01 | Of the earliest transaction     |
| LastDate         | Date        | 2026-12-31 | Of the latest                   |

Collection Books
Description The books the program knows about. Not a container: each is its own
Description file, and this is the list of them that the user has opened.
| DataType | Minimum | Maximum | Notes                        |
| Book     | 0       | 1000    | FilePath is unique            |

Background The two books
Given books are : Books
| Name     | FilePath              | FiscalYearStart | FirstDate  | LastDate   |
| Business | books/business.sadb   | 1               | 2005-06-17 | 2026-01-31 |
| Personal | books/personal.sadb   | 1               | 2017-01-02 | 2025-12-09 |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What belongs to a book, and what does not
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule What is held in a book and what is held for the program
Description The rule is whether the thing is a fact about money or a
Description convenience about working. Facts about money are per book, because
Description mixing them is the mistake two books exist to prevent. Conveniences
Description are shared, because setting them up twice is pointless.
Details The bank profiles are the case worth explaining. The same bank sends
Details the same CSV layout for a business account and a personal one, and
Details matching its headings is a fact about the bank rather than about
Details either book. The mapping from a downloaded account number to an
Details account is per book, because that is a fact about the money.
Examples: BookScope
| Thing                        | Scope   | Why                                        |
| Accounts                     | PerBook | A fact about money                         |
| Transactions and postings    | PerBook |                                            |
| Categories                   | PerBook | The business chart is not the personal one  |
| Payees                       | PerBook | Derived from the transactions of the book   |
| Payee rules                  | PerBook | A business payee takes a business category  |
| Remembered splits            | PerBook |                                            |
| Account mappings for OFX     | PerBook | Which account a downloaded number is        |
| Holdings and prices          | PerBook |                                            |
| Reconciliations              | PerBook |                                            |
| Fiscal year start            | PerBook | A business year need not be a personal one  |
| CSV bank profiles            | Shared  | A fact about the bank, not about the money   |
| CSV header aliases           | Shared  | Likewise                                    |
| Window size and column order | Shared  | A convenience                                |
| The list of recent books     | Shared  |                                             |

Attributes BookScope
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Thing     | String |         |
| Scope     | String |         |
| Why       | String | none    |

Scenario A category in one book is not in the other
Description The business book has categories the personal one has no use for,
Description and the pickers in each offer only their own.
Given books are as previous
Given the chart of accounts of Business is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                     | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses                 | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Consulting Fees | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries       | Expense | false       |
Given the chart of accounts of Personal is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses           | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
When category picker opened in Personal
Then category picker offers : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path               |
| Expenses:Groceries |
# Expenses:Consulting Fees is absent: it is not in this book at all, which is
# different from being hidden.

Scenario A payee rule made in one book does not act in the other
Description The rules are learned from categorising, and a category learned in
Description the business book names a business category. Applying it in the
Description personal book would either fail or create that category there.
Given books are as previous
Given payee rules of Business are : PayeeRules
| Pattern | MatchType | Payee  | Category                 |
| STAPLES | Contains  | Office | Expenses:Consulting Fees |
Given payee rules of Personal are : PayeeRules
| Pattern | MatchType | Payee | Category |
Then payee rules in force in Personal are : PayeeRules
| Pattern | MatchType | Payee | Category |
# No rows.

Scenario A bank profile set up for one book serves the other
Description The headings of a CSV file from a bank are the same whichever
Description account they describe, so the profile is shared and the second book
Description needs no setting up. What is not shared is which account the rows
Description are imported into: that is asked, or taken from the mapping of the
Description book being imported into.
Given books are as previous
Given saved profiles are : SavedProfile
| Name         | Signature                                      |
| Example Bank | Posted Date,Description,Amount,Running Balance |
Given the current book is : CurrentBook Vertical
| Name | Personal |
Given the file contains
"""
Posted Date,Description,Amount,Running Balance
01/15/2024,A SHOP,-45.00,1705.00
"""
When headers matched
Then profile recognised is : ProfileRecognition Vertical
| Name  | Example Bank |
| Asked | false        |

Attributes CurrentBook
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Name      | Text |         |

Scenario An OFX account mapping is per book
Description Two accounts at one bank, one business and one personal, have two
Description different numbers and belong in two different books. A mapping in
Description the wrong book would post business money to a personal account.
Given books are as previous
Given account mappings of Business are : AccountMapping
| BankId    | OfxAccountId | AccountPath     |
| 053000196 | 1111111111   | Assets:Checking |
Given account mappings of Personal are : AccountMapping
| BankId    | OfxAccountId | AccountPath     |
| 053000196 | 2222222222   | Assets:Checking |
Given the current book is : CurrentBook Vertical
| Name | Personal |
When statement imported : OfxStatement Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1111111111 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
Then asked to choose an account for : UnmappedAccount Vertical
| BankId       | 053000196  |
| OfxAccountId | 1111111111 |
| OfxAcctType  | CHECKING   |
| Suggestion   | none       |
# The number is mapped, but in the other book. In this one it is unknown, and
# the import asks rather than reaching into the business book to find it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each book balances on its own
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Every book balances independently
Description The check from Transactions is per book. A book is never balanced
Description by something in another one, which is what makes each set of
Description figures defensible on its own.
Given books are as previous
Given postings of Business are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account                  | Amount   |
| B1  | 2025-01-15 | Assets:Checking          | -45.00   |
| B1  | 2025-01-15 | Expenses:Consulting Fees | 45.00    |
Given postings of Personal are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account            | Amount |
| P1  | 2025-01-16 | Assets:Checking    | -20.00 |
| P1  | 2025-01-16 | Expenses:Groceries | 20.00  |
When each book checked
Then book checks are : PerBookCheck
| Book     | TransactionsChecked | OutOfBalance | Balanced |
| Business | 1                   | 0            | true     |
| Personal | 1                   | 0            | true     |

Attributes PerBookCheck
| Attribute           | Type    | Default |
| Book                | Text    |         |
| TransactionsChecked | Integer | 0       |
| OutOfBalance        | Integer | 0       |
| Balanced            | Boolean | false   |

Scenario A transfer may not name an account in another book
Description A posting names an account, and the accounts of another book are
Description not in scope. There is no syntax for it and no account picker that
Description offers it, so this is what happens if a path is typed in.
Given books are as previous
Given the current book is : CurrentBook Vertical
| Name | Personal |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking          |
| Date     | 2025-03-01               |
| Category | Expenses:Consulting Fees |
| Payment  | 500.00                   |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                              |
| Expenses:Consulting Fees is not an account in this book             |

Scenario Money moving between the two books is two transactions
Description A draw from the business is the case that makes people want one
Description book. It is recorded as two transactions, one in each book, each
Description balanced within its own. Equity is where it goes, because from the
Description point of view of the business it is capital leaving and from the
Description point of view of the household it is not income earned.
Details Nothing links the two records. Making the second is the work the two
Details books cost, and it is a handful of transactions a year against the
Details certainty that no business report includes household spending.
Given books are as previous
Given the chart of accounts of Business is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                | Type   | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking     | Bank   | false       |
| Equity:Owner Draw   | Equity | false       |
Given the chart of accounts of Personal is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type   | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank   | false       |
| Equity:Owner Draw      | Equity | false       |
When draw recorded : OwnerDraw Vertical
| Date       | 2025-04-01 |
| Amount     | 4000.00    |
| FromBook   | Business   |
| ToBook     | Personal   |
Then postings of Business are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account           | Amount   |
| B1  | 2025-04-01 | Assets:Checking   | -4000.00 |
| B1  | 2025-04-01 | Equity:Owner Draw | 4000.00  |
Then postings of Personal are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account           | Amount   |
| P1  | 2025-04-01 | Assets:Checking   | 4000.00  |
| P1  | 2025-04-01 | Equity:Owner Draw | -4000.00 |
Then book checks are : PerBookCheck
| Book     | TransactionsChecked | OutOfBalance | Balanced |
| Business | 1                   | 0            | true     |
| Personal | 1                   | 0            | true     |

Attributes OwnerDraw
| Attribute | Type  | Default    |
| Date      | Date  | 2025-01-01 |
| Amount    | Money | 0.00       |
| FromBook  | Text  |            |
| ToBook    | Text  |            |

Scenario A draw is not income in the personal book
Description It would be counted twice: once as business income when it was
Description earned and again as household income when it was drawn. Equity is
Description nominal, so it moves net worth without entering an income report.
Description This is the same reasoning as market value changes in Investments.
Given books are as previous
Given postings of Personal are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account           | Amount   |
| P1  | 2025-04-01 | Assets:Checking   | 4000.00  |
| P1  | 2025-04-01 | Equity:Owner Draw | -4000.00 |
When report run : ReportSpec Vertical
| GroupBy  | Category   |
| From     | 2025-01-01 |
| To       | 2025-12-31 |
| Interval | Whole      |
Then report rows are : ReportRow
| Account | Level | Amount | IsSubtotal |
Then report total is : ReportTotal Vertical
| TotalIncome   | 0.00 |
| TotalExpenses | 0.00 |
| Net           | 0.00 |
Then net worth is : NetWorth Vertical
| Assets      | 4000.00 |
| Liabilities | 0.00    |
| NetWorth    | 4000.00 |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Opening and working in two books
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Two books may be open at once, in two windows
Description A window is open on one book and every register, report and import
Description in it belongs to that book. Having both open is useful on the day
Description a draw is recorded, and there is no window that shows both.
Given books are as previous
When book opened : BookOpen Vertical
| Name | Business |
When book opened : BookOpen Vertical
| Name | Personal |
Then windows are : BookWindow
| Window | Book     |
| 1      | Business |
| 2      | Personal |

Attributes BookOpen
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Name      | Text |         |

Attributes BookWindow
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Window    | Integer | 0       |
| Book      | Text    |         |

Scenario Opening the same book twice brings its window forward
Description Two windows on one book would let the same transaction be edited
Description in two places, and the second edit would silently win.
Given books are as previous
When book opened : BookOpen Vertical
| Name | Business |
When book opened : BookOpen Vertical
| Name | Business |
Then windows are : BookWindow
| Window | Book     |
| 1      | Business |

Scenario A report names the book it is of
Description Two reports of the same period from two books look alike, and the
Description figures are not comparable. The name is on the report so that a
Description printed one can be told from the other.
Given books are as previous
Given the current book is : CurrentBook Vertical
| Name | Business |
When report run : ReportSpec Vertical
| GroupBy  | Category   |
| From     | 2025-01-01 |
| To       | 2025-12-31 |
| Interval | Whole      |
Then report heading is : ReportHeading Vertical
| Book   | Business              |
| Period | 1 Jan to 31 Dec 2025  |

Attributes ReportHeading
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Book      | Text |         |
| Period    | Text |         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the two books come from
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Each QIF file migrates into its own book
Description The two exports in testdata become the two books, and the
Description migration is run twice. Neither run can see the other, so a
Description transfer whose other half is in the other file is unpaired rather
Description than matched across the two. See ImportQif.
Given no books exist
When qif file migrated into a new book : QifMigration
| SourceFile      | BookName |
| pughkilleen.QIF | Business |
| kenpugh.QIF     | Personal |
Then books are : Books CompareOnly
| Name     | FirstDate  | LastDate   |
| Business | 2005-06-17 | 2026-01-31 |
| Personal | 2017-01-02 | 2025-12-09 |

Attributes QifMigration
| Attribute  | Type | Default |
| SourceFile | Text |         |
| BookName   | Text |         |

Scenario A bracketed account naming the other file is created in this book
Description The business export may name an account that lives in the personal
Description one. The migration cannot reach across, so it creates the account
Description in the book it is building and reports it, as it does for any
Description bracketed name it has not seen. Whether the two should be joined
Description afterwards is a judgement, and it is left to the user.
Given no books exist
Given the current book is : CurrentBook Vertical
| Name | Business |
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Bank
D4/ 1'25
T-4000.00
POwner draw
L[Personal Checking]
^
"""
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When qif file imported
Then accounts created are : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path                     |
| Assets:Checking          |
| Assets:Personal Checking |
Then migration notes are : MigrationNote
| Kind           | Detail                                                        |
| AccountGuessed | "[Personal Checking]" was not in the account list; made Assets:Personal Checking of type Bank |
# In the business book this is an asset: money the business has placed outside
# itself. Recategorising it as Equity:Owner Draw is the user's call, and the
# note is what prompts it.
